ge radius, the vertices in $V_1$ and $V_2$ must be far apart.\n\nAssuming $V_1$ is empty, $V_2$ forms a dominating set. If $|V_2| = k$, then $\\gamma_R(G) \\le 2k$. An upper bound on the radius of a connected graph with a dominating set of size k is sought.\n\n\n**Bounding Graph Radius Using Domination**\n\nConsidering a dominating set of size *k*, the neighborhoods of the dominating vertices must cover the entire graph. The distance between any two dominating vertices $d_i$ and $d_j$ is at most 3 in the main graph, resulting in an auxiliary graph where vertices are adjacent if their distance is at most 3.\n\nBecause the main graph is connected, the auxiliary graph is also connected, establishing a relationship between their connectivity. Considering the auxiliary graph with k vertices, a connected graph has a radius bounded above by k-1. Specifically, in a path graph, the radius is $\\lfloor k/2 \\rfloor$.\n\nSince all vertices are within a certain distance from the dominating set, it is concluded that the radius of G is at most 3 when k=2. A path can be constructed to show a maximum distance.\n\n\n**Analyzing Path Structure & Radius**\n\nPaths were constructed where dominators, $d_i$, covered three vertices each. Therefore, $k$ dominators could cover $3k$ vertices. The radius was calculated for such paths: $\\text{rad}(P_{3k}) = \\lceil (3k-1)/2 \\rceil$.\n\nCases for k=1, k=2, and k=3 were validated, and the Roman domination number for $P_6$ and $P_9$ was examined. The relationship $\\text{rad}(G)+1 \\le \\gamma_R(G)$ holds.\n\nIt was considered whether paths could be made longer.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"roman domination\" radius graph","toolAction":"Searching literature","toolSummary":"Search literature on roman domination and radius"}}]}
{"step_index":69,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:51:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:51:33+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:51:36+02:00\nThe search for \"\"roman domination\" radius graph\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the **Roman domination number** and the **radius of a graph** are two distinct parameters that are often studied in relation to one another when analyzing the structural properties of graphs[1][2].\n\n### Key Definitions\n\n*   **Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_R(G)$):** A Roman dominating function on a graph $G = (V, E)$ is a function $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex $u$ with $f(u) = 0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex $v$ with $f(v) = 2$[3][4][5]. The Roman domination number $\\gamma_R(G)$ is the minimum weight $\\sum_{v \\in V} f(v)$ among all such functions[3][4].\n*   **Radius of a Graph ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** The eccentricity of a vertex $u$, denoted $\\text{ecc}(u)$, is the maximum distance from $u$ to any other vertex in the graph[6][7][8]. The radius of the graph is the minimum eccentricity among all vertices: $\\text{rad}(G) = \\min_{u \\in V} \\{\\text{ecc}(u)\\}$[9][6][10].\n\n### Relationship in Research\nWhile they are independent graph parameters, researchers often compare them to establish bounds or characterize specific graph families[1][2]:\n\n*   **Comparative Studies:** There is literature dedicated to comparing the Roman domination number with other parameters like the radius, total domination number, and distance-2 domination number, particularly in specific graph classes such as trapezoid graphs or trees[9][1][2].\n*   **Distance Variations:** Some research explores \"distance Roman domination,\" where the definition of the function is modified to involve distance constraints (e.g., a vertex with value 0 must be within distance $k$ of a vertex with value 2), which creates a more direct bridge between the concept of radius/distance and Roman domination[11].\n*   **Structural Properties:** Studies frequently examine how the radius (and diameter) of a graph influences or limits the Roman domination number, especially when considering how vertices are distributed across the graph's structure[9][12][13].\n\nSources:\n[1] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHrdmgOele8nB6vXl2hTrJxpjjIlwfeLGLnQRKIbTcUiNHYxI0GYf5RCHsaHAzJVKuQvBdyCxH1dwVISsUYnZw_ykSiH3UrnaGRkXiHpuKrIH_0U5Cec_9sMnUFSYgdud7PhQ==)\n[2] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHrdmgOele8nB6vXl2hTrJxpjjIlwfeLGLnQRKIbTcUiNHYxI0GYf5RCHsaHAzJVKuQvBdyCxH1dwVISsUYnZw_ykSiH3UrnaGRkXiHpuKrIH_0U5Cec_9sMnUFSYgdud7PhQ==)\n[3] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGmpicIo400-aAy7QCJo6sqMoEGgPAUyNtgmPon6KN5a9iegDLpGpXh9LhcXOaOoyYYgDgR6KIL5JBeNja9dNI04pSQS_fjDVddfSiUkEdV0vxKoMiLgu8Eb3M96g==)\n[4] [dergipark.org.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMI9__FOcYsp58XWf_u-xQGU_vJyesg6TkGgoMF1Ln1wuQSXFC5xSFsccM-Tf7-QbVmW_nKkCevMbkkxcmnTj16sc9-UMVBae5RdzS6n9w14tAnbQ2mL_EXiX01zu6KLxwhg2CrZ0Rj-m6AKRRbQ==)\n[5] [dergipark.org.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMI9__FOcYsp58XWf_u-xQGU_vJyesg6TkGgoMF1Ln1wuQSXFC5xSFsccM-Tf7-QbVmW_nKkCevMbkkxcmnTj16sc9-UMVBae5RdzS6n9w14tAnbQ2mL_EXiX01zu6KLxwhg2CrZ0Rj-m6AKRRbQ==)\n[6] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGQaA3MgDysxgPeZ4l-W0yuXOuBsx1jw9GLxEPvkieF5DnnGfqz_4QuI891KLVYJOHFFKFj7pFrVFnRItAh5k1lU13KlwkdfI8V72_b-XiWVoF3c1OsYFg88727nlKUEcGmjM8TPbi4)\n[7] [ijmsi.ir](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH5M8VO0tyiOXQeGPW3-wJX3TBUg07Frmkhr-pjg7mWNW8bVGT6gxHYT0El3wqzl2XHs8QvJBJOuGyUiyfMZGnoZm0rdhfaty2fp7Wtik0dSNL5z682l87-79ffeYUkvvxsTgwklgHCPhCrgV81aaAz93-5)\n[8] [dergipark.org.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEMI9__FOc