{"step_index":75,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:52:01Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:52:01+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:52:05+02:00\nThe search for \"\"roman domination number\" \"diam(G)\" \"rad(G)\"\" returned the following summary:\nIn graph theory, the **Roman domination number**, denoted by $\\gamma_R(G)$, is a parameter that measures the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function on a graph $G$. The **diameter**, $diam(G)$, and **radius**, $rad(G)$, are fundamental distance-based parameters that frequently appear in bounds and relationships concerning domination numbers[1][2][3].\n\n### Key Relationships and Bounds\nWhile there is no single universal equality linking these three parameters for all graphs, they are related through various theoretical bounds and structural properties:\n\n*   **General Distance Inequalities:** By definition, for any connected graph $G$, the radius and diameter are related by $rad(G) \\leq diam(G) \\leq 2 \\cdot rad(G)$[4]. \n*   **Bounding $\\gamma_R(G)$ with Diameter:** Research has explored upper bounds for the Roman domination number in terms of the diameter. For instance, it has been noted that for a graph $G$ with $\\gamma_R(G) > 3$, the diameter can be bounded by a function of the Roman domination number, such as $diam(G) \\leq \\frac{3(\\gamma_R(G) - 3)}{2} + 2$[5].\n*   **Distance-based Domination:** The study of \"distance Roman domination\" often generalizes these concepts. The $k$-distance Roman domination number, $\\gamma_{kR}(G)$, directly incorporates the distance properties of the graph, often involving $diam(G)$ in its definition or as a factor in its upper bounds (e.g., for certain graph classes)[6][7].\n*   **Structural Context:** In many papers, $diam(G)$ and $rad(G)$ are used alongside $\\gamma_R(G)$ to establish extremal results or to characterize specific graph families (like trees or grid graphs) where the Roman domination number can be constrained by the \"span\" or \"depth\" of the graph[8][9][10].\n\n### Summary of Parameters\n*   **Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_R(G)$):** The minimum weight of a function $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex with $f(u)=0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex with $f(v)=2$[1][11].\n*   **Diameter ($diam(G)$):** The maximum distance between any two vertices in $G$[1][3].\n*   **Radius ($rad(G)$):** The minimum eccentricity among all vertices in $G$, where the eccentricity of a vertex is the maximum distance to any other vertex[1][3].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific theorem or inequality (such as a Nordhaus-Gaddum type relation or a specific bound for trees), it is often found in literature discussing \"distance domination parameters\" or \"Roman domination bounds\"[12][11][13].\n\nSources:\n[1] [dergipark.org.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEsR3gfud1-68YTpw45oFQt2_ZwyNouHBNwSQDs5t266Zzv7S8wG8f66mdl19AN9FJZyTz0Ivblj_iSw0HLPzoe37jyd4oZtCwB_VVlOvNR3YMN2NnL6Jr2xj5ObOXfdQz2dcLum5m8QePoMt8zDMI=)\n[2] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHhvhai1yDe0pzQH5jKAQXPIUefpX49cNEwGOUj48FxgfaRiXSFF9IFCCAT-TdYQyxVn9J0rCrAn_NNe_iiyTYqzcTlS4MMQcayRRd_XTuMBV3ds72zbluv4ADFXDdUujcZRpFv-s9R9jzD3DVHWBePA9Dsv4WgoJIGAPWCWQ0kz3Gs1Q==)\n[3] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGBVlFQu1TpCjv7uPmTaU4OTE7nDe7DpcKAN3geeaL8VC8J3d5lutH1UlQrYvY2AUtSTCjzVHA7EAV_qvPEAZ-rvwJIBEO82Ism5s_M-7CXUN_43XWPPIuf8Vvcpbc5MbF8xume0ycymg==)\n[4] [dokumen.pub](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFuVjPNfIR2jANMgg6tbzdp8HL_8ELhsc2oohZ2XOCQFijWpGDYUtYBTSwogY3FLibbvglWQWjCiT1tYgJMBK_g7yMAwFss8YSH2jcJ0I5V9q2huj-1ENoKvYLg9gw5zBEVcAdEmamcrtnNzvOuw4C44EQsEqj66oC7RPcdvwPl4pBnYDOW-wpXk8d-IwyOrUEATNiZXdaexY9hzuzBHvzZNgDwQkqYp4t6lZRTfDefqw==)\n[5] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH_1ri6ync2o6t9Dr_ekdrQd